  "readmeFile": "docs\\README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Features\n- Added opt-in bitonal (1-bit CCITT Group 4) image compression through AdvancedCompressionOptions for smaller and sharper scanned PDFs\n- Added support for digitally signing an existing named signature field using PdfSignature.SignatureFieldName\n- Added configurable Table of Contents titles and optional title entries through ChromePdfRenderOptions\n\nEnhancements\n- Improved tagged PDF handling when merging, importing, or securing PDFs; PDF/UA structure, document language, and accessibility metadata are now preserved for copied content\n\nBug Fixes\n- Fixed an issue where RenderHtmlAsPdfUA could incorrectly mark a wrapped paragraph and following content as artifacts, causing meaningful text to be hidden from assistive technologies\n- Fixed an issue where converting PDFs containing emoji or other transparent content to PDF/A-1 could retain unsupported transparency and fail PDF/A-1b validation\n- Fixed an issue where PDF/UA output could lose the required PDF/UA identification metadata in XMP\n- Fixed an issue where PDF/UA conversion could cause images to disappear after a page was resized\n- Fixed an issue where saving merged documents as PDF/UA could produce content that strict PDF readers could not decode\n- Fixed an issue where signing PDFs could create duplicate AcroForm signature field names; affected signatures are now reported with a warning during verification\n- Fixed an issue affecting PDF processing on macOS Apple Silicon deployments\n- Fixed an issue where a failed PdfDocument constructor could cause an unrelated open document to be closed\n- Fixed intermittent crashes when PDF documents were accessed concurrently\n\nBreaking Changes\n- PdfDocument now supports adding one digital signature at a time; attempting to add a second signature to the same document throws an exception. To add multiple signatures, sign and save the document, reopen it, and then add the next signature\n\nUpdates\n- Updated internal dependencies",
  "requireLicenseAcceptance": true,
  "summary": "IronPDF helps C# Software Engineers to create, edit and extract PDF content in .NET projects.\n\n# C# HTML to PDF for .NET 10, .NET 9, .NET 8, .NET 7, .NET 6, .NET 5, Core, Standard, and Framework\n# Work with PDFs in C# using HTML, MVC, ASPX, and images\n# Generate, Edit, Read and Secure PDF Documents\n\nGenerate PDFs with Pixel Perfect Chrome HTML to PDF Rendering:\nThe IronPDF library takes the frustration out of generating PDF documents by not relying on proprietary APIs. “Html-To-Pdf” renders pixel-perfect PDFs from open standard document types: HTML, JS, CSS, JPG, PNG, GIF, and SVG. In short, it uses the skills that developers already possess.\n\nFully Supports:\n* .NET 10, .NET 9, .NET 8, .NET 7, .NET 6, .NET 5, Framework, Core, and Standard\n* Windows, macOS, Linux, Azure, Docker and AWS\n\nFor code examples, tutorials and documentation visit https://ironpdf.com/",
